win32u: a DPI-scaled layered window hands its per-pixel alpha to the real surface even when its shape is unchanged.

Filmora's installer showed a black promo banner while it downloads. The page loads and renders; on top of it the
installer (DuiLib) lays an owned layered window, "wae_mask", 600x330, painted with UpdateLayeredWindow as white at
alpha 1 (premultiplied 1,1,1,1) plus a minimize button. It catches the mouse while the slides show through. We
showed it opaque, so the banner area was a flat (1,1,1). Found with Ghidra (CMaskWnd, DuiLib's layered paint and
its #01ffffff fill) and NEUTRON_LAYER_TRACE: the mask's driver surface was layered with alpha_mask 0, which the
driver paints opaque, while the shadow windows of the same installer got alpha_mask ff000000.

The installer is not DPI-aware, so at 175% each window surface is win32u's scaling wrapper around the driver
surface. UpdateLayeredWindow marks the wrapper per-pixel (alpha_mask) after the window is already on screen, and
the wrapper passed its layered attributes on only when the shape bitmap changed. DuiLib gives every window a
window region on WM_SIZE, so the shape was already the whole window, and a veil with no fully transparent pixel
gives the same whole-window shape: no change, nothing handed on. The shadows have fully transparent edges, so
theirs changed and got through. The wrapper now hands the attributes on at every flush;
window_surface_set_layered marks the target dirty only when a value differs.

Lab test host (lab/axhost, 32-bit, the banner page in a WebBrowser with the installer's veil done the DuiLib way,
judged by pixel count): with the window region, 178 black 2/2, 178 + this showed the page 2/2, and black again
with the kill switch on the same build. Without the region, or in a DPI-aware process, it showed the page on 178.
The installer itself, 178 + this: the banner shows the carousel with the minimize button on top.

Kill switch NEUTRON_SCALED_LAYERED=0.

--- a/dlls/win32u/dce.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/dce.c
@@ -162,6 +162,14 @@
     return CONTAINING_RECORD( window_surface, struct scaled_surface, header );
 }
 
+/* NEUTRON scaled-layered: kill switch NEUTRON_SCALED_LAYERED=0 (see scaled_surface_flush). */
+static BOOL neutron_scaled_layered(void)
+{
+    static int v = -1;
+    if (v < 0) { const char *e = getenv( "NEUTRON_SCALED_LAYERED" ); v = !(e && e[0] == '0'); }
+    return v;
+}
+
 static void scaled_surface_set_clip( struct window_surface *window_surface, const RECT *rects, UINT count )
 {
     struct scaled_surface *surface = get_scaled_surface( window_surface );
@@ -326,10 +334,19 @@
         HRGN hrgn = map_dpi_region( window_surface->shape_region, surface->dpi_from, surface->dpi_to );
         window_surface_set_shape( surface->target_surface, hrgn );
         if (hrgn) NtGdiDeleteObjectApp( hrgn );
-
+    }
+    /* NEUTRON scaled-layered: the target composites with this wrapper's layered attributes, so hand them on at
+     * every flush, not only when the shape bitmap changed. UpdateLayeredWindow marks the wrapper per-pixel
+     * (alpha_mask) after the window is already on screen. A window with a window region already has a full
+     * shape, and pixels that all have some alpha give the same full shape, so the shape never "changed" and the
+     * target stayed "layered, no attributes", which the driver paints opaque. Filmora's installer: a DPI-unaware
+     * DuiLib veil (white at alpha 1, with DuiLib's WM_SIZE window region) over the promo banner came out (1,1,1)
+     * black at 175%; DPI-aware, or without the region, the page showed through (2026-11-04).
+     * window_surface_set_layered only marks the target dirty when a value differs.
+     * Kill switch NEUTRON_SCALED_LAYERED=0. */
+    if (shape_changed || neutron_scaled_layered())
         window_surface_set_layered( surface->target_surface, window_surface->color_key,
                                     window_surface->alpha_bits, window_surface->alpha_mask );
-    }
     /* NEUTRON dwm-alpha: the target composites the same way */
     if (surface->target_surface->dwm_alpha != window_surface->dwm_alpha)
         window_surface_set_dwm_alpha( surface->target_surface, window_surface->dwm_alpha );
